{
  "_doc": "Shell commands and the action tokens every command classifier must give them: the proxy's actions.rs, @intutic/gate's actions.ts, intutic-clawde's gate/actions.py and the hook gates' hold classifier (gateBody.ts). Each implementation compares only the tokens it produces: the hold classifier knows deploy, publish, release and db_write (and, from gate v23, run_tests and the 3.0 tokens). notHeld commands classify to none of them. Every space in a pattern stands for whatever the shell or the database reads as a word break: SQL_GAP in actions.rs, the phrase matcher (shared-types phrases.ts, phrases.py) everywhere else. adversarial lists [unit, times] inputs every JavaScript and Python implementation must classify in under 200 ms: nothing may backtrack on them. 3.0 added migrate, prod_write, destroy, force_push and payment, emitted after the original eight in that order; DROP and TRUNCATE are both db_write and destroy; force_push is the git push phrase plus a whole-word --force or -f; rm -rf is destroy only when a target is absolute, starts with ~ or contains .. (Q3). falsePositives lists [command, token] pairs the proxy, @intutic/gate and intutic-clawde classifiers must not produce; the hook gates keep the plain update needle until a gate version changes it, so they do not run this list: update counts as db_write only with a set within 8 words, and .environ is not .env. held also pins what that must not lose: a script body is read, not skipped, because a heredoc into a file may run in the same command. Text from echo, printf and git commit is dropped only when the shell parser understood the whole command and nothing consumes that text; a pipe, redirect, group, substitution or control word keeps it.",
